                   DIGITAL EQUIPMENT CORPORATION
                  PERSONAL COMPUTER BUSINESS UNIT
                       BACKGROUND INFORMATION
                           February 1995


Digital Equipment Corporation's Personal Computer Business Unit 
(Digital PC) develops, manufactures, markets, sells and supports a 
complete line of leading-edge personal computer products and 
services to companies, institutions and individuals engaged in all 
manner of business throughout the world.  The company's products 
and services provide PC computing solutions that meet virtually any 
need from home office and small office applications, to power-
hungry computational tasks on the desktop or on the road.  Digital 
PC servers help people work together by providing the power and 
reliability necessary for a complete range of networked 
applications, from basic print and file sharing to mission critical 
enterprise-wide applications.

The Digital PC Business Unit employs 3,500 people worldwide.  With 
headquarters in Acton, Mass., it operates worldwide through three 
major geographic areas.  The Americas Operations, which includes 
all of North, South and Latin America, is also headquartered in 
Acton, Mass.  Europe Operations, which encompasses Western Europe, 
Eastern Europe, North Africa and the Middle East, is headquartered 
in Geneva, Switzerland.  The Asia/Pacific Operations, which 
includes all of Asia and the Pacific Rim, Australia, New Zealand 
and Japan, is headquartered in Hong Kong.  Each regional 
organization maintains its own sales, marketing, and profit and 
loss responsibilities.

BUSINESS STRATEGY SUMMARY

When it was formally established in July 1993, Digital PC set a 
goal to become a key player in the high-volume personal computer 
market as quickly as possible.  Digital plans to achieve this end 
by adhering to a six-point strategy that encompasses:

    -  competitive desktop, server and mobile product lines for all 
       segments of the PC market; 
    -  an indirect channel sales model that focuses on long-term, 
       mutually beneficial partnerships; 
    -  a support and service infrastructure for both customers and 
       resellers; 
    -  high-quality, predictable manufacturing and supply logistics 
       that balance flexibility and cost; 
    -  brand franchise growth through creative marketing, awareness 
       and demand generation programs; and 
    -  an autonomous organization, based on a high-volume, low-cost 
       model.

The success of this broad ranging strategy can be seen in the fact 
that, according to Dataquest, Digital PC risen in the PC industry 
worldwide shipment rankings from #27 in 1992, to #15 in 1993, to 
#11 in 1994.

THE DIGITAL PC PRODUCT PORTFOLIO

Digital PC products provide comprehensive solutions to the entire 
spectrum of general business and office applications and to the 
complex challenges that customers encounter when implementing 
today's client/server computing strategies.  In addition, Digital 
PC is investing in research and development in order to continue to 
innovate across its product line and to bring ever higher levels of 
functionality, leading technical and ergonomic design and maximum 
ease-of-use to its product portfolio.  The company is constantly 
improving every aspect of users' experience with Digital PC 
products and services, beginning with the 'out-of-box' experience 
-- the way in which products are packaged and presented, as well as 
start-up menus and documentation.  

Digital HiNote -- Digital PC has vaulted into the rapidly expanding 
notebook computer market with its HiNote and HiNote Ultra 
computers.  The  HiNote family combines Digital's renowned 
engineering expertise with leading aesthetic and ergonomic design 
for powerful, lightweight and easy to use notebook computers.  
HiNote Ultra redefines the leading edge of the notebook computing 
class by incorporating desktop performance in a four-pound, 
one-inch-high package -- the thinnest notebook computer available.  
The unique fold-down battery and wedge-shaped floppy disk dock 
provide a breakthrough notebook design.  The HiNote family provides 
the ultimate in performance and reliability in a lightweight and 
stylish package, providing mobile workers with desktop computer 
performance and the convenience of a mobile system.

Digital Venturis --  Available in both slimline and full-profile 
configurations, the Venturis line is Digital PC's value-oriented 
desktop family designed to meet the computing requirements of 
general business users in standard office applications, as well as 
those of users in a client/server environment.  Ideal for the 
volume purchaser, Venturis systems are simple to install, easy to 
use and easy to maintain, while providing room to expand as 
computing needs evolve.

Digital Celebris -- The Celebris line is targeted at business 
professionals who demand a balance of performance and price.  The 
product line's enhanced power and flexibility meets more demanding 
desktop computing challenges, such as compute-intensive graphics, 
software development and financial analysis.  Celebris systems 
incorporate high-performance Intel i486 and Pentium processors, PCI 
and IDE for faster system, disk and peripheral performance and S3 
64-bit graphics processing power.  Celebris PCs are upgradeable to 
meet changing computing demands.  

Digital Celebris XL -- The Celebris XL line is comprised of 
Digital's highest-performing desktop computing systems and is 
targeted at the 'power user.' The Celebris XL line offers maximum 
flexibility in power, graphics and memory.  Celebris XL  features 
an upgradeable processor architecture that allows users to protect 
their computing investment by taking advantage of successive 
generations of Intel or Digital Alpha microprocessors.

Digital Prioris Servers -- Digital's full line of PC server systems 
meet the computational needs of workgroups of all sizes.  The 
Prioris MTE Server is an entry-level server ideal for small 
workgroups' file, print and peripheral sharing requirements.  The 
Prioris XL Server can be configured as either a midrange print/file 
server or an entry-level application server.  Prioris XL server's 
scaleable design and advanced architecture enables the addition of 
higher-performance processors and peripherals over the life of the 
product.  The recently introduced Prioris HX family of highly 
scalable application servers can support workgroups numbering in 
the hundreds.  The Prioris HX family is currently available in 
single and dual processor configurations and offers a host of 
fault-tolerance and redundancy features for maximum reliability, 
including ECC memory, Remote Server Manager support, advanced disk 
subsystems with controller duplexing, integrated hot-swap SCSI 
backplanes, and internal or external RAID.

Digital Starion PC -- The Starion line is Digital PC's initial 
entry into the rapidly growing retail sales channel in the U.S. and 
is targeted at the 'ToHo' -- Tiny Office, Home Office - market.  
Starion PCs come equipped with CD-ROM, sound capabilities, and 
other multimedia features.  In addition, a suite of small office 
applications, such as a telephone answering machine, fax modem and 
software, ClarisWorks, CompuServe, and tape backup drives complete 
the Starion package.  These products are covered by one-year, 
on-site warranty service and are available at CompUSA and Sam's 
Club.

DISTRIBUTION

Digital PC is committed to a measured, rational approach to its 
distribution strategy as a means of minimizing channel conflict and 
assuring potential buyers of the widest range of purchasing 
choices.  The strategy is based on mutually beneficial, long-term 
relationships with key channel partners in each geographical 
region.  For example, Digital's partners in the America's include 
MicroAge, Merisel, Tech Data and Gates F/A.  European partners 
include Simec, Computer 2000, Ideal and West Coast.  Digital's most 
prominent partner in the Asia/Pacific region is the Founder Group.  
Today, Digital PC sells over 80 percent of its products through 
channel partners, with a goal of reaching close to 100 percent by 
the end of 1995.

SERVICE AND SUPPORT 

One of Digital PC's greatest strengths is its ability to leverage 
the traditional superiority of Digital Equipment Corporation's 
worldclass, worldwide service and support organization on behalf of 
its desktop, mobile and PC server customers around the world.  A 
three-year limited warranty is standard on all desktop and mobile 
personal computers, while Prioris servers are covered by a three-
year, on-site limited warranty with standard next-day response from 
Digital's MultiVendor Customer Services organization or through a 
Digital Channel partner.  Digital's optional Passport service 
program provides 48-hour express service for the full line of 
HiNote notebook computers in the U.S.

MANUFACTURING AND LOGISTICS

Digital PC's strategy is to maintain high-quality, predictable 
manufacturing and logistics operations that balance cost and 
flexibility.  In order to maintain a high level of responsiveness 
to customer orders, manufacturing and logistics operations are 
located close to major customer in each geographic area, with major 
facilities in Kanata, Canada; Irvine, Scotland; and Tao Yuan, 
Taiwan.  Digital PC also maintains integration centers which 
perform customer-requested modifications, such as option 
installation and factory-installed software loading in Brazil, 
Japan, Hong Kong, Singapore, Australia, and the Peoples' Republic 
of China.  Digital PC strives to lead the industry in cost-
effective manufacturing with systems that ensure optimal inventory, 
delivery and quality control.  The goal is to maintain competitive 
manufacturing costs while retaining predictability, flexibility and 
the ability to respond to rapid market and technological evolution.

MARKETING AND SALES

Digital is investing heavily in its PC communications activities, 
using advertising, public relations, industry events and other 
tools as a means of creating awareness among industry opinion 
leaders and a broader base of potential PC buyers.  A crucial 
component of the company's strategy is to work closely on sales 
and marketing programs with its distributor partners in each 
geographical region.  In this way, marketing programs can be 
tailored to appeal to the demands of individual locales, maximizing 
their attractiveness to local audiences.  In general, Digital is 
working to broaden the brand equity it has achieved with its 
traditional core business customers as it expands into new market 
segments, and as the company grows its PC market share.

MANAGEMENT ORGANIZATION 

The overall business model for Digital PC is that of an autonomous 
company within Digital that is specifically organized to compete 
and win in the personal computer industry.  The vertically 
integrated organization includes product strategy and planning, 
product development and engineering, manufacturing and logistics, 
finance and administration, quality and services, and marketing and 
sales.  To effectively manage this dynamic, growing organization, 
Digital PC is staffed with a balance of talented personnel from 
within Digital's corporate structure and with PC professionals 
recruited from other leading companies in the field -- companies 
like Compaq, Apple, IBM, and Hewlett-Packard.

Bernhard Auer, Vice President and General Manager, Personal 
Computer Business Unit -- Auer is responsible for all aspects of 
the Personal Computer Business Unit worldwide.  Previously, Auer 
had served as vice president and general manager of the European 
operations of the Personal Computer Business Unit.  Before joining 
Digital in 1993, Auer was vice president, managing director of the 
general business group of Compaq Europe.  Prior to his tenure at 
Compaq, Auer spent 23 years with International Business Machines in 
senior line and staff management positions across Europe in 
operations, sales and marketing.

Wim Elfrink, Vice President and General Manager, European 
Operations --  Elfrink joined Digital's Personal Computer Business 
Unit as vice president and general manager of European operations 
in September 1994.  He manages Digital's PC business in Western 
Europe, Eastern Europe, North Africa and the Middle East and is 
responsible for business strategy, sales, marketing, distribution 
and business development.  He reports to Bernhard Auer.  Prior to 
joining Digital, he was European Marketing Director, Personal 
Information Products for Hewlett-Packard, headquartered in 
Grenoble.  Previously, as the H-P Country Manager for the Computer 
Products Organization, he established the European Customer Support 
Center in the Netherlands.  He has also held international 
assignments with worldwide responsibility in both the U.S.  and 
Italy for Rank Xerox and Olivetti, respectively.

Frank Fortunato, Vice President and General Manager, Asia/Pacific 
Operations -- Fortunato is responsible for business strategy, 
sales, marketing, distribution and business development in the 
Asia/Pacific region.  Fortunato joined Digital Equipment Corp. in 
1985 and has held a variety of management positions including, 
director, desktop systems for the PC Business Unit's Asia/Pacific 
area, where he played a key role in bringing the first Digital PCs 
to that region.  He has more than 25 years of international 
experience in the information technology industry, including 
positions with Simplex Corp., Elscint, Inc. and International 
Telephone and Telegraph (ITT).  He holds a master's degree in 
electrical engineering from Brooklyn Polytechnical Institute and a 
bachelor of science degree in physics from Adelphi University.

Raymond L. Weadock, Vice President and General Manager, Americas 
Operations -- Weadock was promoted to this post in July 1994.  He 
is responsible for business strategy, sales, marketing, 
distribution and business development for the Americas region.  
Prior to joining Digital in February 1994, Weadock had been vice 
president of the worldwide distribution products group for Conner 
Peripherals Corp., where he also served as vice president of 
Americas distribution sales.  Previously, he was vice president and 
chief operating officer of Plus Development Corp., which later 
merged with Quantum Corp.  He has also served as director of 
worldwide sales for Visicorp Corporation.  Weadock is a graduate of 
the Executive Institute at Stanford University's Graduate School of 
Business and holds a bachelor of science degree in business 
administration from the University of Arkansas.

Winifred S. Briney, Vice President, Mobile Product Line -- Briney 
is responsible for Digital PC's mobile product line including 
product management, product development, engineering and product 
marketing worldwide.  She joined Digital in September 1993 as 
marketing director for the U.S. operations of Digitals Personal 
Computer Business Unit.  Previously, Briney had spent 26 years with 
International Business Machines, most recently as vice president, 
marketing, for IBM's independent company, WFS Workforce Solutions.  
She also has held a series of senior positions with IBM, including 
vice president, personal systems product marketing and vice 
president, personal systems marketing support for the National 
Distribution Division.  Briney holds a bachelor of arts degree in 
philosophy from Manhattanville College.

Duane R. Dickhut, Vice President, Server Product Line -- Dickhut 
was named vice president of  Digital PC's server product line in 
August 1994.  He is responsible for the PC unit's server product 
family, including product management, product development, 
engineering and product marketing worldwide.  Previously, Dickhut 
had been vice president, Product Management and Development for the 
PC Business Units and was responsible for all product management 
and engineering for the desktop, server and mobile product 
families.  Dickhut joined Digital in 1971 as a field service 
engineer.  He has held a variety of senior management positions 
within the company in the systems engineering, semiconductor 
engineering, manufacturing, marketing and customer service 
organizations.  Prior to joining Digital, he was a logic design 
engineer with General Motor's AC Electronic Division.  Dickhut has 
a master's degree in business administration from Northeastern 
University and a bachelor of science degree in electrical 
engineering from the Milwaukee School of Engineering.

Howard D. Elias, Vice President, Desktop Product Line -- Elias 
joined Digital's Personal Computer Business Unit in August 1994.  
He is responsible for Digital PC's desktop and deskside product 
families, including product management, product development, 
engineering and product marketing worldwide.  Previously, Elias was 
vice president of worldwide marketing and general manager of the 
desktop product line for AST Research, Inc. in Irvine, Calif.  
Prior to AST, he had spent more than 17 year with Tandy Corporation 
in a variety of management positions, including vice president of 
sales and marketing; president (interim) GRID Systems; and vice 
president, corporate marketing.  Elias attended Wayne State 
University and Lawrence Institute of Technology.

John McClelland, Vice President, Worldwide Manufacturing and 
Logistics -- McClelland is responsible for the worldwide 
manufacturing and logistics functions of Digital's PC business.  
Prior to joining Digital in February 1995, McClelland was vice 
president, worldwide manufacturing and logistics for the IBM PC 
Company.  He began his 26-year career with IBM in the European 
finance organization and moved to manufacturing and logistics in 
1980.  During his IBM tenure he held a variety of  executive 
manufacturing management positions in the United Kingdom, Europe, 
Africa and the Middle East.  McClelland is a visiting professor and 
fellow of the University of Paisley in Scotland, a companion of the 
British Institute of Management, a fellow of the Royal Society of 
Arts, Manufactures and Commerce, a member of the Confederation of 
British Industry - National Manufacturing Council, and a director 
of the Scotland Quality Foundation.  In 1994, he was appointed a 
Commander of the Order of the British Empire in recognition of his 
contribution and services to the advancement of British high 
technology manufacturing and to education.

John J. Millerick, Vice President, Finance --  Millerick is 
responsible for all aspects of Digital PC's worldwide financial 
management, including financial control, accounting and management 
information as it relates to overall administration, manufacturing, 
engineering, and sales and marketing.  Prior to joining Digital, 
Millerick was with Wang Laboratories where he held a number of 
senior positions in the U.S. and abroad including corporate 
controller and acting CFO.  Previously, he was president of 
Publishers Design and Production Services, Inc. and has also held 
several management positions at Arthur Andersen & Co.  Millerick 
holds a bachelor of arts degree in mathematics from College of the 
Holy Cross and a master's degree in business administration from 
the Amos Tuck School of Business Administration at Dartmouth 
College.

Arthur J. O'Donnell, Vice President, Quality and Customer Services 
-- O'Donnell is focused on maintaining maximum customer 
satisfaction and product/service cost competitiveness.  His 
responsibilities include worldwide establishment, implementation 
and maintenance of a quality system across all PC business 
functions.  In addition he is responsible for development and 
management of a comprehensive customer care and support capability, 
as well as managing the warranty budget on behalf of the product 
lines.  O'Donnell joined Digital in 1974 and has an extensive 
background in customer services and quality management.  Most 
recently he was vice president of Digital's Multivendor Customer 
Services Hardware Systems Business Services.  O'Donnell is a 
graduate of Framingham State College and Boston University's 
Development Program.  He holds an MBA from Northeastern University.
 
