            DIGITAL EQUIPMENT AND SAFEWARE FORM STRATEGIC
           HARDWARE PARTNERSHIP; DELIVER FIRST OF ITS KIND
                 MULTIMEDIA MEDICAL EDUCATION SYSTEM


    Digital PCs Provide Platform for Medical Training Application


MAYNARD, Mass. -- February 27, 1995 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
and SafeWare Inc. today announced a partnership and agreement to 
sell SafeWare's Virtual Physician software with Digital personal 
computers.  The Virtual Physician, a PC-based multimedia medical 
education application, allows doctors and medical professionals to 
simulate emergency patient care in a three-dimensional emergency 
department using life-like patients.

     Under terms of the agreement, Digital will supply all hardware 
for individual and multi-user versions of The Virtual Physician and 
provide PC support.  The Digital PC/SafeWare multimedia educational 
system will be available from SafeWare this spring.   

     "We expect The Virtual Physician to be to medical education 
what flight simulators have become to airline pilot training," said 
Brian Keaton, MD, vice president, Medical Operations at SafeWare. 
Dr. Keaton is a leading specialist in emergency medicine, an 
educator, and active practitioner at a teaching hospital in Akron, 
Ohio. In addition to Dr. Keaton, SafeWare's Medical Advisory Board 
includes several nationally prominent physicians, including Mark 
Smith, MD, Fellow of the American College of Emergency Physicians 
(FACEP), chair of the Department of Emergency Medicine at the George 
Washington University Medical Center in Washington, D.C., and Earl 
Smith, MD, FACEP, who has been responsible for establishing national 
and federal clinical guidelines in emergency medicine.

     SafeWare also has formed an alliance with the Ohio Chapter of 
the American College of Emergency Physicians (ACEP) to provide 
content and subject matter expertise. 

     Each product in the Virtual Physician series will present a 
patient with a particular symptom.  Cardiac Arrest Resuscitation 
Exercise (CARE), the first module in the series, gives attending 
physicians, medical students, residents, and EMTs the ability to 
simulate the management of cardiac arrest using three-dimensional 
patients who respond to treatments in a realistic manner.  Each 
product offers an introductory lecture incorporating sound, video, 
photographs, x-rays, and animations.  The package also includes 
multimedia multiple choice questions and answers that are 
accompanied by explanations and references.

     According to Skip Gladfelter, Americas marketing vice president 
for Digital's Personal Computer Business Unit, "The Virtual 
Physician is a dramatic way to introduce Digital's multimedia 
capability to the physician on the front line.  This partnership 
combines the strength of Digital's personal computers and acclaimed 
support organization with SafeWare's unique medical application.  
This continues our tradition of service to the medical community."

     CARE will be available with Digital Venturis and Digital 
Celebris desktop systems in a minimum configuration of 486/66 Mhz 
with a 14.4 Kbs modem to access medical databases.  Dennis M. 
Flanders, Sr., president and CEO of SafeWare said: "We chose Digital 
PCs because of Digital's reputation for quality products, award 
winning service, and upgrade capabilities. It is critical that our 
client physicians receive systems that are not only easy to use and 
powerful, but are also supported by standard-setting field service."  

     Wyle, Inc., one of Digital's largest distributors, will handle 
the systems integration and distribution of the combined 
hardware/software solution.

     SafeWare was founded in 1993.  SafeWare's first computer-based 
training software, Security Awareness from Education (SAFE), is used 
by more than one-million people in government agencies and 
corporations concerned with the security of computer information and 
software piracy.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace.
