           DIGITAL INTRODUCES STORAGEWORKS RAID ARRAY 410
                   SUBSYSTEM FOR UNIX ENVIRONMENTS


  High Performance and Competitive Pricing Provide Optimal Storage
        Solutions for Hewlett-Packard, IBM and Sun Platforms


MAYNARD, Mass. -- January 31, 1995 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today introduced the newest member of its StorageWorks RAID 
subsystem family, the StorageWorks RAID Array 410 for multi-platform 
UNIX environments.  Based on performance, capacity, and price, the 
StorageWorks RAID Array 410 surpasses other RAID arrays in the UNIX 
market and provides high reliability, robust fault tolerance and a 
scalable, modular architecture.

     In a separate announcement today, Digital's Storage Business 
Unit introduced a range of new disk drives and enclosures for 
multiple computing platforms.

     According to Paul Feresten, vice president of sales and 
marketing for Digital's Storage Business Unit, "The StorageWorks 
RAID Array 410 is optimized for multiple platform UNIX environments 
running mission-critical, transaction processing applications.  Now, 
networking and I.S. managers can purchase a fully-qualified RAID 
solution with the flexibility and scalability required to meet 
future storage needs."

     Robert Rennick, vice president of Digital's Storage Business 
Unit, added, "The new StorageWorks RAID Array 410 provides storage 
solutions for multiple platforms.  No company offers as broad and 
deep a line of storage subsystems as Digital -- backed up by our 
worldwide service and support."

StorageWorks RAID Array 410 -- High-Performance RAID For Multiple 
UNIX Platforms

     The StorageWorks RAID Array 410 for Hewlett-Packard 9000, IBM 
RS/6000, and Sun SPARC systems provides reliable, expandable, 
cost-effective RAID storage.  The RAID Array 410 is optimized to 
provide fast and highly reliable data access for heavy-duty, 
transaction processing applications in industries such as banking, 
communications, financial services, and insurance.  Because the 
product uses controller-based RAID, users can harness the power of 
